{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "aspects_discussed",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.95,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "This column directly answers the query -- categorizing which film aspects (acting, cinematography, plot, music, effects, overall_quality) each reviewer focuses on gives a strong axis to group and compare. However, groundedness is shaky. Row IMDB-0001 focuses on writing/dialogue and general badness, yet 'cinematography' is asserted with no visual-craft mention. IMDB-0002 tags 'cinematography' from a passing 'camera pans' line -- weak but arguably defensible. IMDB-0004 tags 'cinematography' where the text says 'atmospheric and well-made' (borderline warranted). There is a recurring pattern of over-attributing 'cinematography', suggesting the extraction inflates that aspect beyond what the text supports."
    },
    {
      "name": "num_aspects",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.6,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A simple count derived from aspects_discussed. It offers a modest new axis (breadth of review focus) and is not merely a restatement since it enables aggregation on review scope. Groundedness inherits from the parent column: when aspects are over-attributed, the count inflates (e.g., IMDB-0001 counts 4 including an unsupported aspect). The '0' for the silent IMDB-0005 row is correct behavior."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
  "source_groundedness": 0.6,
  "rationale": "Both columns are well-targeted to the query 'what aspects are reviewers focusing on' -- aspects_discussed provides the core categorical decomposition and num_aspects a useful breadth measure. The main weakness is groundedness: 'cinematography' in particular is repeatedly asserted for rows whose text discusses writing, plot, or general quality without any visual-craft content (notably IMDB-0001), and num_aspects propagates that inflation. Silent rows are handled correctly with none/0, which is appropriate and not penalized."
}

